## [0.2.0] — Blue-dominant palette + hero rebuild

Three changes requested after review of 0.1.x.

### 1. Palette flipped to match ting.com exactly

The client supplied a screenshot of ting.com showing the vivid blue section
treatment and asked for those exact shades. That resolves the hierarchy question
flagged in 0.1.0 in favour of ting.com's own usage: **blue is dominant, coral is
the secondary highlight.**

Rather than repeat the earlier mistake of hard-coding a palette decision across
fifteen components, the accent hierarchy now lives behind semantic tokens in
`app/globals.css`:

```css
--color-brand-*   ->  var(--color-tblue-*)    /* #3253ff family — dominant */
--color-accent-*  ->  var(--color-coral-*)    /* #ff8c6c family — highlight */
```

Components reference `brand-*` / `accent-*` and never the raw `tblue-*` /
`coral-*` scales. Re-theming the entire site is now repointing two blocks.

Applied:

| Surface | Was | Now |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Primary CTA buttons | coral, ink text | **blue `#3253ff`, white text** |
| ZIP "Check" button | blue | ink-90 (neutral, so the blue CTA owns conversion) |
| Eyebrows / section kickers | coral-70 | brand-70 `#243edb` (light) · brand-40 `#84aeff` (dark) |
| Feature checkmarks | coral-70 | brand-60 |
| Promo qualifiers | coral-90 | brand-70 |
| FAQ open state, step bubbles, pillar icons | coral | brand |
| Wordmark "ting" | ink-90 | brand-60 — matches ting.com's own blue logotype |
| "Most popular" badge | coral | **kept coral** — mirrors Ting's own coral "Recommended" flag |
| Hero eyebrow dot, disclosure dot | coral | **kept coral** |
| Form error state | coral-80/90 | **Ting's official `--error-*` scale** (`#e54949`, `#aa1c35`) |

Contrast re-checked: blue `#3253ff` with white text is 5.5:1 (AA); brand-70
`#243edb` on white is 7.5:1; brand-40 `#84aeff` on navy-90 is 9.0:1.

### 2. Hero rebuilt

The previous hero was a split layout with a dark pricing card on the right —
the client called it generic. Studied the two reference retailers:

- **kineticfiber.us** — one rounded dark panel, price lockup **inline in the copy**
  with a small label beside it, trust chips in a strip below.
- **bestfiberinternet.us** — full-bleed dark hero, two-tone headline with the
  second line in brand colour, ZIP checker in a contained panel, trust chips as
  a bar across the bottom.

Both put the price *in the copy flow* and neither uses a floating spec card.
The rebuild takes that structure:

- Full-bleed `navy-90` section with two soft `#3253ff` radial washes and a
  masked dot field for depth — no illustration, no motion, no 3D.
- Two-tone H1: white + `brand-40`, driven by new `HERO.headlineAccent` in
  `lib/content.ts` so the split stays data-authored.
- **Price rail**: `HERO.priceLabel` + `<PriceLockup size="lg" tone="dark" />` +
  spec chips derived from the plan (`formatSpeed`, `dataPolicy`, `contractTerm`).
  The pricing card is gone.
- One white ZIP card carrying both required hero actions side by side.
- Trust chips as a bordered strip flush to the hero's bottom edge.

### 3. Removed the stacked CTA block

Removed the standalone "Call to order" button and its
"Talk to a person, confirm your address and order in one call." paragraph from
under the ZIP checker, as requested.

**Constraint note:** the original brief states the hero must keep BOTH a ZIP
checker and a call button, and that the hero call button must not be removed.
To honour both instructions, the hero retains exactly **one** "Call to order"
button — relocated into the ZIP card beside the Check action — and the
explanatory paragraph is gone entirely. If the intent was to drop the hero call
button altogether, deleting the `action` prop on `<ZipChecker>` in
`components/Hero.tsx` does it, but that would contradict the brief.

### Also fixed
- Header showed the phone number twice at ≥1280px (a text `PhoneLink` plus the
  button, from an `xl:inline-flex` rule). Removed the redundant text link; the
  header now carries one phone element per breakpoint.
- `ZipChecker` gained an `action` slot and a card shell; it no longer imports
  `CallButton` itself.

### Verification after the change
```
npm run lint    0 errors, 0 warnings
npm run build   compiled clean, TypeScript passed, / prerendered static
```
- 320px viewport — `scrollWidth` 320, **0** overflowing elements
- 12 `tel:` links, **0** missing `data-call-cta`
- Hero contains the ZIP checker **and** exactly one "Call to order" button
- Plan CTAs: "Call to order" ×4, "Call for pricing" ×1 (Business Fiber)
- No raw phone numbers as link text outside the header and footer
- Section order unchanged: `top · plans · bundles · mobile · fine-print · why · how-it-works · faq`
- Forbidden-phrase scan clean; 0 external links

