## [0.4.0] — Photography added

Seven Gemini-generated images delivered by the client, processed and wired in.

### Processing
| Step | Detail |
| --- | --- |
| Watermark | Gemini stamps a sparkle at a fixed offset from the bottom-right (~137→92px from the right, ~149→96px from the bottom, independent of output size). Measured it, then cropped past the cheaper edge per image — bottom for the portrait cards, right for the landscape ones. Verified clean via a corner contact sheet. |
| Format | PNG → JPG, quality 84–88, mozjpeg. **17.7 MB → 1.25 MB.** Next re-encodes to WebP/AVIF at request time. |
| Hero resolution | First pass cropped the hero's bottom, leaving it 1408×603. The hero box is ~760 CSS px tall (~1140 device px at DPR 1.5), so the browser upscaled it ~1.9×. Re-cropped from the **right** edge instead to keep the full 768px height, then upscaled to 2000×1221 (lanczos3 + sharpen). Now downscales rather than up. |

### Placement — all driven from `lib/content.ts`
A new `SiteImage` interface (`src`, `alt`, `width`, `height`, optional
`position`) carries every image. No component hard-codes a path.

- **Hero** — `HERO.backgroundImage`, full-bleed via `next/image` `fill` with
  `priority`. Below `lg` a flat 88% scrim; from `lg` a left-to-right ramp
  (96% → 88% → 55%) so the headline stays on near-solid navy while the house
  reads on the right.
- **Why Ting** — `WHY_US.pillars[].image`, 4:5 cards. The four line icons were
  removed in favour of photographs, matching how ting.com uses imagery on its
  own homepage. `FiberIcon`, `SymmetryIcon`, `PriceTagIcon`, `UnbundleIcon` and
  `PILLAR_ICONS` deleted from `Icons.tsx` as dead code.
- **Bundle** — `SERVICE_META.bundle.image`. `ServiceSection` now renders a
  two-column editorial block when a single-plan line carries an image, instead
  of one card stranded in a wide grid. Still generic: set `image` on any
  single-plan service line and it picks up the same treatment.
- **How It Works** — `HOW_IT_WORKS.cta.image`, two-column closing block.

`WHY_US` lost its `as const` and gained an explicit `Pillar[]` type — the const
assertion narrowed each pillar's image to its own literal, so only the one
carrying `position` type-checked.

### Contrast, measured not assumed
Sampled the actual hero pixels under the text band in Node, composited the
scrim over them and computed worst-case ratios. First pass came back **4.26:1**
— fine for the large headline but a fail for the normal-size subline. Fixed by
raising the ramp midpoint 82% → 88%, the right end 45% → 55%, and lifting the
subline from `navy-20` to `navy-10`:

| Text | Worst case | Needs | |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Headline (large) | 5.30:1 | 3:1 | PASS |
| Subline (normal) | 4.97:1 | 4.5:1 | PASS |
| Trust chips (normal) | 5.30:1 | 4.5:1 | PASS |

### Verification
```
npm run lint    0 errors, 0 warnings
npm run build   compiled clean, TypeScript passed, / prerendered static
```
- 320px with all 7 images — `scrollWidth` 320, **0** overflowing elements
- 7 images, 0 broken; 1 decorative (hero, `alt=""`), 6 with descriptive alt
- Loading strategy: 1 eager + `priority` (hero, the LCP element), 6 lazy
- 11 `tel:` links, 0 missing `data-call-cta`; hero still has 0 call buttons
- Plan CTAs unchanged: "Call to order" ×4, "Call for pricing" ×1
- Forbidden-phrase scan clean; no year stamp; section order unchanged

### Note
The dev server's browser cache served stale optimized variants throughout
testing. Confirmed the server itself returns the correct bytes by fetching
`/_next/image?...&w=1920` with `cache: 'reload'` — 1920×1172, ratio 1.638,
matching the new crop. A hard refresh clears it locally; production is unaffected.

