## [0.6.0] — Bundle overlay fixed, eight compliance pages added

### 1. Bundle section overlay — this was a real bug

Two scrims were stacked on the bundle background: a flat `bg-navy-90/86` and a
vertical `from-navy-90 via-navy-90/70 to-navy-90`. They compound:

```
mid    0.86 + (1-0.86) x 0.70 = 0.958
top/bot 0.86 + (1-0.86) x 1.00 = 1.000   <- fully opaque
```

The photograph was almost entirely hidden. Replaced with a single top-weighted
treatment, `bg-navy-90/45` plus `from-navy-90/72 via-transparent to-navy-90/45`:

| Band | Composite | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Top (heading) | 0.846 | white heading 5.09:1, navy-10 subheading 4.77:1 — both pass |
| Middle (card + surrounds) | 0.450 | photo retains 55% of its strength |
| Bottom | 0.698 | grounds the section edge |

Measured against this specific image, whose windows blow out to pure white
(luminance 1.000) — that worst-case pixel is what sets the top-band figure. The
dark-tone subheading was lifted from `navy-20` to `navy-10` to clear 4.5:1.

### 2. Eight compliance pages

Clarified scope: the request was for policy pages, not additional service
sections. Built as data in `lib/legal.ts` → rendered by `components/LegalPage.tsx`
→ routed through a single `app/[slug]/page.tsx`.

| Route | Covers |
| --- | --- |
| `/privacy-and-data-protection` | Collection, use, sharing, rights, retention, security, children |
| `/disclaimer` | Retailer status, pricing/availability limits, speed caveats, liability |
| `/cookies-policy` | Categories, third parties, browser control, GPC handling |
| `/tcpa-policy` | Consent, revocation, internal DNC, calling hours, call recording |
| `/trademarks` | Provider mark ownership, nominative use, no implied affiliation |
| `/marketing-policy` | Claim accuracy, material terms, comparisons, channels, partners |
| `/service-fulfillment` | Order flow, who does what, installation, equipment, billing |
| `/pci-dss` | No card data on this site, telephone order handling, scope |

`generateStaticParams` builds all eight at compile time; `dynamicParams = false`
means any other path 404s rather than rendering an empty document. Adding a
document to `LEGAL_DOCS` creates its route, metadata and footer link — the route
file needs no edit.

Page layout: dark masthead with the aurora field, a sticky numbered table of
contents, numbered sections with a readable 72ch measure, and a closing block
with the retailer disclosure and a call CTA.

### 3. Multi-page plumbing
- `Footer` moved from `app/page.tsx` into `app/layout.tsx` so it renders on every
  route.
- All in-page anchors changed from `#plans` to `/#plans` so they resolve from a
  policy page. The wordmark now links to `/` rather than `#top`.
- **`SmoothScroll` click handler rewritten.** It previously grabbed every
  `a[href^="#"]`; with absolute anchors it would either miss them or hijack
  cross-page navigation. It now resolves the URL and only intercepts when the
  pathname matches the current page, ignoring modified clicks, non-left clicks
  and `target` links. Cross-page links fall through to Next's router.
- Skip link retargeted from `#top` (home only) to `#main-content`, added to both
  `main` elements.
- Header and footer internal links converted to `next/link` for client-side
  navigation.
- `metadataBase` set from `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` so per-page canonicals resolve.
- Footer legal row rewired from four `#legal` placeholders to the eight real pages.

### 4. US spelling
Ten British spellings had crept into the new policy copy (honour, enquiry,
dialling, cancelled, licence). Swept `lib/` and `components/`; none remain.

### Verification
```
npm run lint    0 errors, 0 warnings
npm run build   11 routes, 8 policy pages prerendered as static HTML
```
- All eight routes return **200** with correct per-page titles
- `/not-a-real-page` returns **404**
- 320px on a policy page — `scrollWidth` 320, **0 genuinely overflowing elements**
- Table of contents renders, footer carries 17 internal links

### ⚠ Before these pages go live
1. **Fill in `COMPANY` in `lib/content.ts`.** `legalName`, `addressLines`,
   `privacyEmail`, `legalEmail` and `websiteDomain` are bracketed placeholders
   and render **literally on the pages**.
2. **Have counsel review the text.** These are drafted to industry-standard
   retailer practice, not legal advice. The PCI DSS page in particular states a
   scope position — confirm it matches your actual order-taking process and any
   attestation you hold.
3. **Set `effectiveDate`** on each doc if you want dates shown; the field exists
   and renders only when populated.
4. Set `NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL` in the deploy environment.

